    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="2" relname="preparation">Intuitiveness must be one of the most overused and meaningless terms in the marketing industry.</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="12" relname="span">Many hardware and software vendors state that their product is intuitive and easy to use,</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="10" relname="list">but what are they basing that statement on?</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="10" relname="list">How do they know the product is intuitive and who it is intuitive for?</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="10" relname="list">Is it intuitive for all users, or just expert users?</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="11" relname="span">I think that in many cases they dont know (or care),</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="6" relname="elaboration">which illustrates the point (made by P11) that the term is often used to sell products rather than design them.</segment>
        <group id="8" type="span"/>
        <group id="9" type="span" parent="8" relname="span"/>
        <group id="10" type="multinuc" parent="11" relname="evidence"/>
        <group id="11" type="span" parent="9" relname="span"/>
        <group id="12" type="span" parent="9" relname="antithesis"/>
    </body>
</rst>
